[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"HQL (Hibernate Query Language) Fundamentals"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate Query Language (HQL) is an object-oriented query language, similar to SQL, but operating on persistent objects and their properties rather than directly on database tables and columns."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Between SQL and HQL"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SQL operates on database tables, foreign keys, and columns. HQL operates on persistent classes, object associations, and properties."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HQL queries are fully object-oriented and support concepts like inheritance, polymorphism, and association."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HQL is database-independent, meaning Hibernate translates HQL into the specific SQL dialect of the underlying database."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Select Query in HQL"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To retrieve all instances of an entity, you specify the entity class name instead of the table name. Here is an example of fetching all records for an Employee entity."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"String hql = "FROM Employee";
Query<Employee> query = session.createQuery(hql, Employee.class);
List<Employee> results = query.getResultList();"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the WHERE Clause"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can filter results using the WHERE clause. Note that properties of the Java class are referenced, not the database column names."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"String hql = "FROM Employee E WHERE E.department = 'IT'";
Query<Employee> query = session.createQuery(hql, Employee.class);
List<Employee> results = query.getResultList();"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Parameters in HQL"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent SQL injection and improve query execution performance, always use named parameters instead of string concatenation."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"String hql = "FROM Employee E WHERE E.salary > :minSalary";
Query<Employee> query = session.createQuery(hql, Employee.class);
query.setParameter("minSalary", 50000.0);
List<Employee> results = query.getResultList();"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Pagination in HQL"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate provides built-in methods on the Query interface to handle result set pagination efficiently."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"Query<Employee> query = session.createQuery("FROM Employee", Employee.class);
query.setFirstResult(0); // Offset
query.setMaxResults(10); // Limit
List<Employee> employees = query.getResultList();"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"HQL bridges the gap between object-oriented domain models and relational databases, providing a robust, database-agnostic mechanism for data retrieval and manipulation."}]
